# Harper's Hedging rule matches three phrases, so this list carries
# the rest. `should work` and its kin count only after `it`, `this`, or
# `that`, where they hedge a claim about the code (`this should fix
# the race`). After `you` they give advice, which the
# technical-writing skill writes with `should`. `should help` stays
# out, because the allows rule reports `help you`. `in a way` stays out
# too, because it also names a manner (`in a way that keeps the
# order`). The Optional rule of the same name holds `relatively`,
# `effectively`, `virtually`, and `practically`. The opinion rule holds
# the phrases that frame a claim as someone's view. `kind of` and `sort
# of` count only before an adjective, adverb, or verb and without a
# determiner before them, because docs use them as nouns (`the kind of
# diff`, `a sort of index`). `a bit` is left out, because a mask sets a
# bit. `a little bit` stays. `possibly` is left out too, because it gives the range of a
# value (`a non-nil, possibly empty map`). `rather` is left out as
# well, because `rather than` is a contrast and a `!than` guard misses
# it when a comment line breaks between the two words.
#
# `appears to` skips a determiner, pronoun, or proper noun after it,
# where it means `becomes visible to` (`the prompt appears to the
# user`).
#
# `fairly` and `quite` count only as degree words, before an
# adjective, an adverb, or (for `quite`) a determiner (`fairly
# standard`, `quite a large scope`). Before a verb, `fairly` means
# justly (`a normalizer that ignores whitespace can fairly ignore line
# breaks`).
expr main [somewhat, arguably, perhaps, probably, maybe, (in essence), (!DET [kind, sort] of [ADJ, ADV, VERB]), essentially, basically, (a little bit), (to some extent), (to a certain extent), (more or less), (generally speaking), (for the most part), (in most cases), (it seems), (it seemed), (seems to), (seem to), ([appears, appear] to ![DET, PRON, PROPN]), (tends to), (tend to), presumably, (fairly [ADJ, ADV]), (quite [ADJ, ADV, DET]), apparently, seemingly, supposedly, ostensibly, (in theory), (in principle), (in a sense), ([it, this, that] should [work, fix, resolve, suffice]), ([it, this, that] should be [fine, enough, safe, ok, okay])]
let message "Hedge. State the claim or drop it."
let description "Flags words that soften a claim without adding information."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The printer takes the kind of each segment from the token."
allows "You should fix the typo before you merge."
allows "The cache evicts entries in a way that keeps the order."
allows "The mask sets a bit for each enabled flag."
allows "The walker visits the child rather than the alias."
allows "A sort of index maps each anchor to its node."
allows "What kind of error does it return?"
allows "Load returns a non-nil, possibly empty map."
allows "The prompt appears to the user after login."
allows "A normalizer that ignores whitespace can fairly ignore line breaks too."
